#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Expose Linux: macOS Exposé / Mission Control for a Wayland desktop (wlr-layer-shell, Umbriel).

One full-screen overlay over the blurred desktop with every window of every desktop as a live thumbnail, as big as
they can be while they all fit. Keyboard first: arrows move, Enter opens the window, Esc closes, typing letters
picks windows whose title or app starts with them (the others dim). The mouse works too: click a window to go to it,
middle-click (or the x on a card) to close it, click the background to leave. The pointer disappears the moment the
keyboard is used, and hovering selects only after the mouse really moves.

The pictures come from `expose-shots` (ext-image-copy-capture, one frame of each window, also those on other desktops
or scrolled out of view); the window list, focusing and closing go through `umbriel` (JSON window list, the actions
window-focus:<id> and window-close:<id>). Colours come from the active Noctalia palette, the font from the GTK setting,
the blur from the compositor (Umbriel layer rule for the namespace "expose-linux").

It runs in the background with no tray icon; starting it again toggles it.

    expose-linux               open / close it (starts the background program when it is not running)
    expose-linux --background  only start the background program (login)
    expose-linux --list        print the windows it would show
    expose-linux --quit        stop the background program

Alt+Tab: `expose-toggle next` / `prev` (bind them to Alt+Tab / Alt+Shift+Tab). The first press opens it; further Tab
presses while Alt is held step through the windows in reading order (Shift: backwards); letting go of Alt then goes to the
selected window at once. A plain Alt+Tab and release leaves it open (arrows + Enter, or click).

Needs: python-gobject, gtk4, gtk4-layer-shell, and `expose-shots` (built by install.sh).
"""
